{
  "_comment": "One settings page for every edition; an edition locks keys (docs/decisions/0043-one-settings-page-an-edition-locks-a-key.md). Run by workers/app/test/hosted_shared_settings.test.js against the hosted Worker and by tests/test_shared_settings_cases.py against TriageServer. `answer_keys` and the entry keys are GET /api/settings' shape on both. `editions` is what each answers for `locked` and `limits`: TriageServer as `cyris triage-ui` starts it, which answers its own limits whatever it locks, and the hosted Worker as its policy file computes them, for a tenant the registry allows one digest time. `writes` go to POST /api/settings/values and `schedules` to POST /api/settings/schedule, on the hosted Worker and on a TriageServer given the hosted edition's locks; `max_digest_times` is the tenant's maximum in the registry, which TriageServer has no part in. Each case's `status` and `answer` are both servers' answer, or `self_hosted` is TriageServer's where the editions' own limits differ; `refuses` in place of `answer` names the field a 400 is about, worded by each server's own rule. A refused write writes nothing; a saved one `reads_back` from GET /api/settings. `route_locks` are a route that saves several keys, on a server locking `locked` and holding `stored`: it refuses only a change to a locked key, run on TriageServer for each route and on the hosted Worker for the routes it answers. `pins` are the values a hosted run takes from a tenant's rows, held by vitest to the Worker's `hostedValues` and by pytest to `pin_tenant_settings`.",
  "answer_keys": [
    "clef_ready",
    "embedding_providers",
    "fields",
    "languages",
    "limits",
    "locked",
    "missing",
    "providers",
    "sent_as_shown",
    "starting",
    "values",
    "writable"
  ],
  "provider_keys": ["configured", "default_model", "env_var", "name"],
  "field_keys": ["controls", "label", "route", "tab"],
  "editions": {
